FLEX on OS-9/68K -- read this before you conclude flex is broken================================================================flex 2.3 (Vern Paxson), from EFFO forum disk 17.  GNU-style licence inDOC/flex/copying.  It pairs with the `yacc' already on this disk.TWO THINGS WILL STOP YOU, AND NEITHER IS A BUG1.  /dd/tmp MUST EXIST.        flex: can't open temporary action file /dd/tmp/flex000003    flex writes its action file to tmpnam(), which on OS-9 lands in /dd/tmp,    and falls back to a hardcoded "/dd/tmp/flexXXXXXX".  It does not create    the directory.  This disk ships an empty /dd/tmp for exactly this reason.    If you run flex against a different system disk, makdir /dd/tmp first.2.  USE THE SKELETON ON THIS DISK, NOT THE ONE ON YOUR SYSTEM DISK.    flex builds its output by filling in a skeleton file, and looks for    /dd/lib/flex.skel by default.  There are two incompatible skeletons in    circulation.  The older one begins        #include "flexskeldef.h"    and that header is in no distribution we have found -- scanners built    from it will not compile:        lex.yy.c : line 3 **** can't open /dd/DEFS/flexskeldef.h ****    The skeleton in /dd/LIB/flex.skel on THIS disk is the self-contained one    that came with the flex source, and it works.  If flex picks up the wrong    one, name the right one explicitly:        flex -S/dd/LIB/flex.skel myscanner.lA COMPLETE WORKED EXAMPLE, VERIFIED ON THIS DISK    Given t.l:        %%        [0-9]+      printf("NUMBER(%s)\n", yytext);        [a-zA-Z]+   printf("WORD(%s)\n", yytext);        [ \t\n]+    ;        .           printf("PUNCT(%s)\n", yytext);        %%        main(){ yylex(); return 0; }        int yywrap(){ return 1; }    then:        flex t.l                 -> writes lex.yy.c        cc lex.yy.c -qm=16k -f=scan        scan <myinput    On the input "abc 123 hi!" this prints:        WORD(abc)        NUMBER(123)        WORD(hi)        PUNCT(!)    That is the exact sequence used to verify the binary on this disk.    The skeleton includes <stdlib.h>, which K&R Microware C does not have.    /dd/DEFS/CC carries a minimal one; add -V=/dd/DEFS/CC if your scanner    fails to find it.BUILDING FLEX ITSELF    Source is in /dd/SRC/flex, with its own makefile.  Be warned: compiling    dfa.c faults Microware's cpp under cc -- see SOURCES.txt, "A defect in    cpp".  The binary here is the one EFFO shipped, and it is trap-free    (no cio), which is why it is in CMDS and carries no star.